\subsection{Reward-Accuracy Decoupling}
\label{sec:decoupling}

\input{tables/tab2_decoupling}

Table~\ref{tab:decoupling} compares SFT accuracy with GRPO accuracy ($N{=}1{,}319$, greedy decoding).
Across most configurations, GRPO increases reward without improving reasoning accuracy. The exception is CKA-only, which shows a modest $+$0.46\,pp gain (63.91\% $\to$ 64.37\%), consistent with its superior recovery capacity.
The unpruned model achieves 81.27\% after both SFT and 100 GRPO steps (1{,}072 of 1{,}319 problems produce identical answers), despite reward rising from 0 to 0.669.
Standard-pruned accuracy drops 0.38\,pp after GRPO (59.29\% $\to$ 58.91\%); \rasp{} drops 0.84\,pp (61.49\% $\to$ 60.65\%).
The decoupling is bidirectional: Standard peak reward drops 4$\times$ (0.081 $\to$ 0.019) with only 0.38\,pp accuracy loss.
Under GSM8K's binary reward with greedy decoding, GRPO's learning signal captures formatting properties rather than reasoning correctness.


\subsection{Out-of-Distribution Transfer (MATH-500)}
\label{sec:ood}

To test whether the decoupling generalizes beyond GSM8K, we evaluate on MATH-500~\citep{hendrycks2021math}, a harder benchmark spanning algebra, geometry, and number theory.
GRPO reward optimization degrades OOD reasoning even for unpruned models: the dense model drops from 73.6\% (base) to 69.0\% after the full SFT$+$GRPO pipeline ($N{=}500$, $\Delta{=}{-}4.6$\,pp from base; SFT trains on GSM8K and does not target MATH-500).
Pruning amplifies this effect: Standard drops from 26.0\% (SFT) to 25.25\% ($\Delta{=}{-}0.75$\,pp), and \rasp{} shows a marginal gain from 24.2\% to 24.86\% ($\Delta{=}{+}0.66$\,pp).
CKA-only GRPO evaluation is ongoing; CKA-only SFT reaches 39.0\% ($N{=}100$, preliminary), substantially above Standard and \rasp{}.
OOD degradation under GRPO is a general property of in-distribution reward optimization, not specific to pruned models.\footnote{Standard and RASP MATH-500 evaluations are 80--85\% complete; final values may shift by $\pm$1--2\,pp.}

