\subsection{GRPO Responsiveness and Scale}
\label{sec:decoupling}

\input{tables/tab2_decoupling}

The recovery capacity gradient manifests in accuracy as well as reward.
Table~\ref{tab:decoupling} compares SFT and GRPO accuracy ($N{=}1{,}319$, greedy decoding).
CKA-only is the only pruned method where GRPO improves accuracy ($+$0.46\,pp, 63.91\% $\to$ 64.37\%), consistent with its active learning dynamics.
Standard drops 0.38\,pp (59.29\% $\to$ 58.91\%); \rasp{} drops 0.84\,pp (61.49\% $\to$ 60.65\%).
The unpruned model maintains 81.27\% through both stages (1{,}072 of 1{,}319 problems produce identical answers), while reward rises from 0 to 0.669: under GSM8K's binary reward with greedy decoding, GRPO captures formatting properties rather than reasoning correctness.

OOD evaluation on MATH-500~\citep{hendrycks2021math} ($N{=}500$, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500}) confirms that reward-accuracy decoupling is a general property of in-distribution reward optimization, not specific to pruning.
The dense model drops from 73.6\% (base) to 69.0\% after SFT$+$GRPO ($\Delta{=}{-}4.6$\,pp; SFT trains on GSM8K).
Among pruned models, CKA-only SFT reaches 29.0\% (Table~\ref{tab:math500}), substantially above Standard and \rasp{}, preserving the recovery capacity ordering on a harder benchmark.\footnote{Standard and RASP MATH-500 evaluations are 80--85\% complete; final values may shift by $\pm$1--2\,pp.}
